  <LessonChrome slug="honor-and-compassion">
    <header class="page-head">
      <h1>Honor completed by compassion</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Today we add compassion to the foundation. The first layer closes here:
        courage, loyalty, a name worth keeping, and pity for an enemy.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="learn" aria-labelledby="will-learn">
      <h2 id="will-learn">What you’ll learn</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Achilles keeps dragging Hector’s body. Apollo says he has flung aside all pity. Zeus orders the body given back for a ransom.</li>
        <li>Priam goes to the ships, clasps Achilles’ knees, and kisses the hands that killed his sons.</li>
        <li>They weep together, Priam for Hector, Achilles for his father and for Patroclus. Achilles speaks of two urns on the floor of Zeus’s palace.</li>
        <li>The warrior now has courage, loyalty, a name, and pity. He still lacks the self-command and judgment the next module will add. The poem ends with Hector’s funeral, not with the fall of Troy.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <div class="lesson-body">
      <p class="scene-today">Today we add compassion to the foundation.</p>
      <h3>The gods are angry</h3>
      <p>
        Let’s begin from the dragging, which has not stopped. Achilles binds
        Hector’s body behind the chariot and hauls it round the tomb of Patroclus.
        Butler says he dishonours the body shamefully. The blessed gods look down
        in pity. On the twelfth morning Apollo speaks. The gods ought to be
        ashamed. Hector burned thigh-bones for them. Achilles has flung aside all
        pity, and all that conscience which, Butler says, “at once so greatly
        banes yet greatly boons him that will heed it.” A man may lose a son or a
        brother and still, in the end, let go. Achilles will not.
      </p>
      <p>
        Zeus does not pretend the honours are equal. Hector was dearest to the
        gods of all who lived in Ilius, because the offerings never failed. He
        sends Thetis to tell her son that the gods are angry, and he himself more
        angry than the rest. Achilles is to give the body up and take a ransom.
        Achilles answers, “So be it.” If Zeus commands it, the man who brings the
        ransom may bear the body away. The limit Lesson 6 could not find inside
        honor is given here as a command. Notice that it comes from outside him.
      </p>

      <h3>The father in the tent</h3>
      <p>
        Iris tells Priam to go alone, with one servant to drive the mule-wagon,
        and with gifts that will satisfy Achilles. Hermes — Butler’s Mercury —
        will escort him. Achilles will not kill a suppliant; he will entreat him
        with honourable courtesy. Priam loads vestments, cloaks, rugs, ten talents
        of gold, tripods, cauldrons, and a cup he does not grudge. Hecuba is
        afraid. He goes anyway. He says that if he is to die at the ships, he
        would still first take his son in his arms.
      </p>
      <p>
        He enters unseen and goes straight to Achilles. He clasps the knees and
        kisses the dread murderous hands that had slain so many of his sons. That
        is the sentence the syllabus asks you to know. Achilles thinks of his own
        father. He takes the old man’s hand and moves him gently away. The two
        weep. Priam, at Achilles’ feet, weeps for Hector. Achilles weeps now for
        his father and now for Patroclus, until the house is full of the sound.
        When the grief has had its fill, Achilles raises the old man, in pity for
        his white hair and beard.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then he speaks of the urns. On the floor of Zeus’s palace stand two urns,
        one of evil gifts and one of good. The man for whom Zeus mixes them meets
        now good and now evil. The man who receives only evil is scorned, hunted
        by famine, and unrespected by gods and men. So it befell Peleus, and so,
        the speech goes on, it has befallen Priam. Shared grief, and a common
        mortality: that is what the two men recognize. It is not a treaty. It is
        pity.
      </p>

      <h3>What the warrior has, and what he lacks</h3>
      <p>
        Achilles has the body washed and laid on a bier, out of Priam’s sight, so
        the father will not see his son and then break into anger that Achilles
        might answer by killing him. They eat. Priam marvels at Achilles’ beauty.
        Achilles marvels at Priam’s presence. Priam asks for a bed. He has not
        closed his eyes since those hands took his son’s life. The poem gives him
        the night. In the morning the body goes home. Andromache leads the lament.
        The <em>Iliad</em> ends with a funeral, not with the fall of the city.
      </p>
      <p>
        Count what the first layer now contains. Courage: Hector before the
        gates, and Achilles back in the war. Loyalty: Patroclus, and the grief
        that ended the withdrawal. A name worth keeping: <em>timē</em> and
        <em>kleos</em>, the prize and the song, from the first lessons. And pity
        for an enemy, which Book 22 did not have and Book 24 does. The outline is
        exact about what is still missing. Self-command and judgment are not yet
        his. Achilles obeys Zeus. He does not yet govern himself. The Odyssey, in
        the next module, is where the course adds mind and a household. This poem
        stops when the body is burned and the lament has been sung.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the work you already do, the close is practical. A code of reputation
        can make you brave and can make you savage, and it cannot bury the
        quarrel. Pity for the person on the other side of the damage is the step
        that lets a funeral happen. It is not yet wisdom. It is the last stone of
        the foundation, and the next age has to build on it because this one
        failed where wrath had no inner limit.
      </p>
    </div>

