    <section class="takeaways" aria-labelledby="takeaways-title">
      <h2 id="takeaways-title">Key takeaways</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Nothing in the earlier weeks gave Achilles a reason to stop dragging the body. The command comes from Zeus, after Apollo names the missing pity.</li>
        <li>Priam’s kiss is the poem’s highest human act in this book: the father of the dead man at the knees of the killer.</li>
        <li>The two urns put both men under one mortality. Shared grief is the compassion the layer was missing.</li>
        <li>The first layer is complete, and it is not enough. Courage, loyalty, a name, and pity still need the self-command and judgment of the next module. The poem ends with Hector’s funeral, not with Troy’s fall.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="summary">
        Honor began as a prize and a wound. It ends, in this poem, as a man who
        can weep with his enemy and give the body back. The wrath that could not
        limit itself is why the next age has to teach a mind and a household.
      </p>
    </section>

